tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="right">11</td><td align="right">42</td><td align="right">2</td><td align="right">26</td><td align="right">19</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x000a0;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Pakistan</td><td align="left">WB Index</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="right">23</td><td align="right">24</td><td align="right">11</td><td align="right">22</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="right">37</td><td align="right">a</td><td align="right">23</td><td align="right">21</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x000a0;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Tanzania</td><td align="left">WB Index</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">21</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="right">40</td><td align="right">a</td><td align="right">15</td><td align="right">16</td><td align="right">29</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="right">40</td><td align="right">a</td><td align="right">16</td><td align="right">19</td><td align="right">25</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x000a0;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Uganda</td><td align="left">WB Index</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="right">18</td><td align="right">21</td><td align="right">23</td><td align="right">19</td><td align="right">20</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="right">18</td><td align="right">21</td><td align="right">21</td><td align="right">20</td><td align="right">20</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p><italic>Notes</italic>: due to rounding off, the rows may not add up to 100%; a due to clustering of households into large groups, no households are categorised in this wealth category.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>The categorisation of the households into wealth groups was sensitive to the measure of economic status used (Table <xref ref-type="table" rid="T4">4</xref>). The results are only shown for Indonesia and Uganda, the countries for which a refined stratification could be made using all the indices. For these two countries, on average 27% of the households was categorised into a different quintile when using Index 1. Largest changes were observed when using Index 2 and 3, where on average about 47% of the households had shifted to another quintile. Most of these households moved to an adjoining quintile, while on average 10% of all households moved to a further quintile. When considering all ten countries, at least 18% of the households was categorised into a different quintile when using Index 1 (results not shown).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T4"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Change of households to other wealth groups when using alternative indices as compared to WB Index</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Country</td><td align="left">Wealth Index</td><td align="center">% in same wealth group</td><td align="center">% moving 1 wealth group</td><td align="center">% moving 2 or more wealth groups</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Indonesia</td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="center">73</td><td align="center">27</td><td align="center">0</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="center">53</td><td align="center">38</td><td align="center">9</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="center">50</td><td align="center">37</td><td align="center">13</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Uganda</td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="center">72</td><td align="center">24</td><td align="center">3</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="center">56</td><td align="center">35</td><td align="center">9</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="center">54</td><td align="center">36</td><td align="center">10</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p><italic>Note</italic>: due to rounding off, the rows may not add up to 100%</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>For almost all countries, the magnitude of inequality in mortality was sensitive to the use of at least one of the alternative indices (Table <xref ref-type="table" rid="T5">5</xref>). For four of the ten countries, we observed a minor (10&#x02013;30%) change in RII, while for five other countries the change was substantial (&#x0003e;30%). While for five countries inequality decreased when using alternative indices, for some (2) countries inequality increased compared to the WB index, and for others (2) there was a mixed pattern of an increase in inequality when using some alternative indices, and a decrease in inequality when using others.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T5"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Under-5 mortality rates per wealth group and Relative Index of Inequality (RII)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td></td><td></td><td align="center" colspan="5">Under-5 mortality rates (per 1,000 live births)</td><td></td><td align="center" colspan="2">95% CI for RII</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td colspan="5"><hr></hr></td><td></td><td colspan="2"><hr></hr></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Country</td><td align="left">Wealth Index</td><td align="right">Poorest</td><td align="right">Second</td><td align="right">Middle</td><td align="right">Fourth</td><td align="right">Richest</td><td align="right">RII</td><td align="right">low</td><td align="right">up</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Bolivia</td><td align="left">WB Index</td><td align="right">135.1</td><td align="right">106.0</td><td align="right">93.4</td><td align="right">39.8</td><td align="right">32.2</td><td align="right">6.30</td><td align="right">4.76</td><td align="right">8.34</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 1</td><td align="right">134.6</td><td align="right">103.0</td><td align="right">96.0</td><td align="right">45.0</td><td align="right">28.8</td><td align="right">6.04</td><td align="right">4.58</td><td align="right">7.97</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 2</td><td align="right">130.1</td><td align="right">99.3</td><td align="right">94.2</td><td align="right">44.1</td><td align="right">32.7</td><td align="right">5.89</td><td align="right">4.43</td><td align="right">7.83</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 3</td><td align="right">126.2</td><td align="right">120.7</td><td align="right">92.4</td><td align="right">46.4</td><td align="right">32.7</td><td align="right">5.29 +</td><td align="right">4.02</td><td align="right">6.96</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x000a0;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Brazil</td><td align="left">WB Index</td><td align="right">89.4</td><td align="right">52.5</td><td align="right">36.2</td><td align="right">26.1</td><td align="right">29.3</td><td align="right">6.41</td><td align="right">4.02</td><td align="right">10.22</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Index 